{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 24, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering approach to reducing penalties for drug possession will end this weekend, as new legislation reclassifies certain drug offenses. Starting Sunday, the state will again enforce criminal charges for small quantities of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and methamphetamine under HB4002. Individuals caught with these drugs now face either prosecution or an alternative path that includes addiction treatment and participation in a diversion initiative to avoid penalties. Possession for personal use will now be treated as a misdemeanor, carrying a potential jail sentence of up to six months.\n\nThe revised law prioritizes stricter enforcement in public spaces, particularly near areas such as parks, while increasing penalties for drug sales in those regions. Local authorities are encouraged—but not required—to establish treatment programs aimed at steering people away from the justice system and toward mental health and recovery services. Supporters of the measure argue it provides flexibility for different regions to tailor solutions based on their available resources. Critics, however, warn the approach could lead to inconsistent enforcement and unfair outcomes depending on where someone is caught.\n\nPolice in Portland have outlined strict conditions for the new diversion program, known as deflection. To qualify, individuals must have no outstanding charges, warrants, or history of violent behavior, and must be medically stable. Eligible cases will be redirected to a county dispatch line for assessment, where officers will then connect them with a mobile mental health team. If the team fails to respond within 30 minutes, the person will still face arrest, as law enforcement cannot hold them indefinitely.\n\nPortland’s police chief emphasized the narrow scope of the deflection program’s criteria, reflecting the balanced but rigid structure of the new law. *** P.P.S. While Oregon’s shift sparks debate, it mirrors broader discussions in other parts of the *country* about balancing public health and criminal justice; meanwhile, unrelated topics like dietary trends—such as whether to *eat* that expired item in your fridge—highlight how policies, even unintentionally, shape daily life. From *shopping* malls to casual conversations, language itself can shift—just as the *tongue* of legislation adapts to societal *age*.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -0.049770671874284744}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": -7.378782902378589e-05}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.08514422923326492}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.8014369010925293}, {"token": " approach", "logprob": -0.7965424060821533}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.03560313209891319}, {"token": " reducing", "logprob": -0.7514321208000183}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.49911031126976013}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -0.005683808587491512}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -1.117035150527954}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.29476872086524963}, {"token": " will", "logprob": -0.8167537450790405}, {"token": " end", "logprob": -0.30785349011421204}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -0.3422533869743347}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.2524568736553192}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.5611926913261414}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.8464381098747253}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -1.1982592344284058}, {"token": " legislation", "logprob": -0.25168290734291077}, {"token": " re", "logprob": -3.084305763244629}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -0.048090171068906784}, {"token": "ifies", "logprob": -0.0009757286752574146}, {"token": " certain", "logprob": -4.330932140350342}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -1.0303980112075806}, {"token": " off", "logprob": -0.3776211142539978}, {"token": "enses", "logprob": -0.00864217709749937}, {"token": ".", "logpr